   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the town law, in relation to the acquisition of real property\nby eminent domain",
    "basePrintNo": "S1343",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1343-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§64 & 215, Town L",
    "lawSection": "Town Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1343\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the town law, in relation to the acquisition of real\nproperty by eminent domain\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to afford all Towns and Improvement\nDistricts throughout New York State conditional condemnation enabling\nthem to take the same approach in acquiring private utilities as now\nenjoyed by the water authorities in City of New York\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. amends section 64 of the town law by adding a new subdivision\n2-b which provides 'conditional condemnation' to towns.\n\nSection 2. amends section 215 of the town law by adding a new subdivi-\nsion 5-a which provides 'conditional condemnation' to improvement\ndistricts.\n\nSection 3. is the enacting clause.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn the 1990s, legislation was enacted that granted water authorities\nmuch more flexibility in an eminent domain/condemnation proceeding seek-\ning acquisition of a private water utility. Water authorities were\ngranted the power to conduct a \"conditional condemnation\".  Simply stat-\ned, if the condemnation judge sets a valuation much higher than antic-\nipated, the water authorities can walk away without cost.  \"Conditional\ncondemnation\" is not currently available to Article 13 improvement\ndistricts or Article 12 town water districts.\n\nThe original statute establishing the Water Authority of Western Nassau\nindicates that it was initially formed in 1990 SCWA was established much\nearlier than that.\n\nPlease note that the original enabling legislation for Western Nassau\nprovided \"basic condemnation\" rights under Section 1198 f subdivision 5:\nPowers of the authority. This subdivision 5 parallels subdivision 5 of\nTown Law Section 215 which governs the condemnation powers of the Massa-\npequa Water District (\"MWD\"). It also parallels subdivision 2 of Town\nLaw Section 64 governing the condemnation rights of the Town of Hemp-\nstead Water Department. All three paragraphs provide \"simple\" condemna-\ntion under the Eminent Domain Proceedings Law (\"EDPL\").\n\nIn \"Basic\" condemnation, under the EDPL, once a condemnation \"map or\nfiling\" is made in Supreme Court, there is no turning back. Legal title\nvests immediately in the municipality. The only thing remaining to do\nfor the condemnation judge is to determine a final valuation (subject to\nappeal) which establishes the \"fair value\" to be paid for the condemned\nproperty.\n\nWater authorities recognized that \"basic or simple\" condemnation works\nwell if the property sought is a water easement or even two acres for a\nnew well. \"Fair market\" valuations are fairly straight-forward for these\nbasic acquisitions. However, substantial valuation difficulties arise\nwhen the water authorities seek to acquire a private water system\ncomprised of land, equipment, facilities and the \"intangible component\nof a going business\". By virtue of these complex issues, no one could\naccurately predict what a condemnation judge would do.  Consequently,\nduring the early 1990s, Water Authorities persuaded the Legislature to\ngive them a right to undertake a \"conditional condemnation\". This would\n\"balance\" the right of the private utility to get a fair price. At the\nsame time, title would not automatically vest at the filing of the\ncondemnation proceeding under the EDPL.\n\nInstead, these new statutes give the authority the right to \"withdraw\"\nfrom the condemnation proceeding- without cost- should the ultimate\nvaluation by the condemnation court prove to costly. See, Saratoga Water\nServices v. Saratoga County Water Authority, 83 NY2d, 205 (Ct. App .\n1994) at 212.\n\nModeled after General City law Section 20(2), the Saratoga Court of\nAppeals affirmed the constitutionality of Saratoga's conditional condem-\nnation legislation. The conditional condemnation language approved by\nthe Court of Appeals is now included in two separate (identical) stat-\nutes enacted for Western Nassau (1992) and for SCWA (1994) (both\nenclosed as Exhibit E). Please note that these standalone statutes were\nenacted after the authorities were originally established. Conditional\ncondemnation authority is also incorporated directly in the enabling\nlegislation for the North Shore Water Authority at page 20, section\n114-S (copy enclosed as Exhibit F, see last page).\n\nFrom all of the above, if MWD is to entertain a formal condemnation of\nthe Eastern Massapequa portion of the NYAW service area and/or if the\nTown of Hempstead Water Department also entertains a formal condemnation\nof the southwestern portion of the NYAW service area, both need similar\n\"conditional condemnation\" legislation which would allow them to with-\ndraw from the proceeding if the valuation goes too high.\n\nThis legislation should be adopted as it benefits all Towns and Improve-\nment Districts throughout New York State enabling them to take the same\napproach in acquiring private utilities as now enjoyed by the water\nauthorities in City of New York.\n\nFinally, the \"conditional condemnation\" should be provisioned upon a\nsuccessful permissive referendum required under Town Law Section\n209-e(3) conducted after the Town Board approves the annexation of the\nnew area to be serviced by either municipality.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
